<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wars of the Diadochi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/323-bce">323</a>–<a href="/years/281-bce">281 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diadochi">Diadochi</a> were the conflicts fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s generals, the Diadochi or successors, for control of his empire after his death, lasting from 323 to 281 BCE. They ended the unity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a>. Out of them emerged the Hellenistic kingdoms, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-diadochi.jpg" alt="Wars of the Diadochi" width="320" height="154" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This vector image includes elements that have been taken or adapted from this file · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diadochi_LA.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> died on 10 June 323 BCE, leaving an empire from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> valley and no clear successor. The Argead family consisted of his mentally disabled half-brother Arrhidaeus, his unborn son Alexander IV, his reputed illegitimate son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>, his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a>, his sister Cleopatra and his half-sisters Thessalonike and Cynane.</p><p>Two factions formed, one led by Meleager, who supported Arrhidaeus, and one by the cavalry commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas">Perdiccas</a>, who wished to wait for the birth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roxana">Roxana</a>'s child. They agreed that Arrhidaeus would rule as Philip III jointly with the child if it were male. Perdiccas became regent, then had Meleager and other opponents murdered, and in the partition at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> his supporters became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a>: Ptolemy received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, Antigonus Phrygia, Lycia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamphylia">Pamphylia</a>, and Lysimachus Thrace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>News of Alexander's death inspired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamian-war">Lamian War</a>, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and other cities besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> at Lamia until Craterus defeated the Athenians at Crannon on 5 September 322 BCE. In the First War, from 321 to 319 BCE, Antipater, Craterus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> formed a coalition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas">Perdiccas</a>, who was assassinated by his own generals, Peithon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus</a> and Antigenes, after a failed attempt to cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>.</p><p>At the Treaty of Triparadisus Antipater became regent and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> strategos of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. In the Second War, from 318 to 316 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> made himself dominant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and Antigonus defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-of-cardia">Eumenes of Cardia</a>, who was executed after the battle of Gabiene.</p><p>In the Third War Antigonus faced an alliance of Ptolemy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a>. Seleucus secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> after the Battle of Gaza, and Cassander had the young Alexander IV and his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roxana">Roxane</a> murdered, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argead-dynasty">Argead dynasty</a>. By the Fourth War, Antigonus and Demetrius had taken the crown, followed by Ptolemy, Seleucus, Lysimachus and Cassander. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ipsus">Battle of Ipsus</a> in 301 BCE Seleucus arrived in time to crush Antigonus, who was killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Ipsus established the Ptolemies and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> as the two major dynasties of the Hellenistic world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> died in 298 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> passed through the hands of Demetrius, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> and Pyrrhus. Seleucus defeated and killed Lysimachus at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-corupedium">Battle of Corupedium</a> in 281 BCE, leaving himself the only living successor among the original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diadochi">Diadochi</a>, and was assassinated soon afterwards by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-keraunos">Ptolemy Ceraunus</a>. Gallic tribes then rampaged through Macedon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and Demetrius's son Antigonus Gonatas emerged as ruler of Macedon in 277 BCE. Fifty years after Alexander's death Ptolemy II ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, Antiochus I the Asian territories and Antigonus Macedon and most of Greece.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wars of the Diadochi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Appointed satrap of Egypt in the settlement at Babylon, Ptolemy secured his position during the <b>Wars of the Diadochi</b>, famously diverting Alexander’s funeral cortège to Egypt, and proclaimed himself king in 305 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
